From: William Emerison Six <billsix@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 03:25:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 308/488] soh: name the 8 address-named symbols defined in
 z_obj_warp2block.c

SoH carries the Ocarina of Time decomp, in which several thousand symbols are
still named after their ROM address. This commit names every such symbol DEFINED
IN ONE FILE, so the work can be reviewed a file at a time.

The file names the unit; it does not bound it. Each rename is applied wherever
the symbol occurs -- the declaration in the header, the definition, and every
call site -- so this commit reaches into whatever other files use these symbols
and leaves no dangling reference. It is a pure rename: no behaviour changes, and
the only edits that are not renames are the provenance comments described below.

Two name sources are used, and every name says which it came from:

  * zeldaret/oot -- SoH's decomp IS zeldaret/oot at the same ROM addresses, so
    where oot has already named a symbol, that name is adopted verbatim. The
    definition gets a "Name from zeldaret/oot" comment and every declaration a
    short "// was <addr> [oot]" tag.

  * DEDUCED -- oot leaves a great many symbols address-named too, so those
    names are inferred from the body and its callers, and are marked as OURS
    rather than passed off as upstream. The definition gets
    "LLM generated name (HIGH|GUESS), was <addr>: <reason>" and the short tag
    reads "[LLM:HIGH]" or "[LLM:GUESS]". A GUESS is tagged at every call site
    as well, not only the declaration, so a reader who meets the name anywhere
    knows it is inferred. "git grep '[LLM:'" lists every place the decomp
    leans on an inference.

The justification recorded for each individual name follows, one entry per
symbol, in the order the renames were made. Nothing is summarised away: these
are the per-commit reasons from the unsquashed history, with the boilerplate
that was identical in every one of them hoisted into the text above.

All 8 are defined in soh/src/overlays/actors/ovl_Obj_Warp2block/z_obj_warp2block.c.

  * func_80BA1ECC -> ObjWarp2block_IsPlayerInRange
    deduced, confidence HIGH: returns 1 when the player is close enough to
    either block of the pair and standing clear of both footprints, and 0
    otherwise; it is the gate on offering the ocarina.

  * func_80BA2218 -> ObjWarp2block_WaitForOcarina
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the first of the two ocarina sub-states: while
    the player is in range it flags them as near an ocarina actor and, once
    they start playing, opens free play and moves to the song check.

  * func_80BA228C -> ObjWarp2block_CheckSongOfTime
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the second ocarina sub-state: returns 1 once
    the Song of Time has been played and its short countdown has elapsed,
    and drops back to waiting when the ocarina closes.

  * func_80BA2304 -> ObjWarp2block_RunOcarinaCheck
    deduced, confidence HIGH: calls whichever ocarina sub-state is
    installed, then records the last played song for the next frame's
    comparison.

  * func_80BA24E8 -> ObjWarp2block_SetupFindPartner
    deduced, confidence HIGH: installs the action that looks for this
    block's partner.

  * func_80BA24F8 -> ObjWarp2block_FindPartner
    deduced, confidence HIGH: scans the item-action actor list for the other
    Obj_Warp2block sharing this switch flag and adopts it as a child,
    swapping the pair immediately when the flag is already set; after 60
    frames without a match it complains and kills the actor.

  * func_80BA2600 -> ObjWarp2block_SetupActive
    deduced, confidence HIGH: installs the main action once the partner has
    been found.

  * func_80BA2610 -> ObjWarp2block_Active
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the main action: on a successful Song of Time
    it spawns the warp effect, arms the attention cutscene and, twelve
    frames later, swaps the block with its partner.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
